A support line replaces its IVR menu with a voice agent. The demo sounds great: fast, natural, barely a pause between the caller's question and the agent's answer. Three weeks into production, a caller reports that the agent "wouldn't shut up" — it kept talking over her while she tried to correct a wrong account number. The team pulls the trace. The run completed. The transcript is accurate. The LLM call succeeded. Every span is green.

Nothing in that trace explains what the caller experienced, because nothing in that trace recorded when the user started speaking relative to when the agent's audio actually stopped. That gap — between what a text-agent trace captures and what a spoken conversation needs — is the subject of this guide.

{% trace-scenario title="Why did the agent talk over the interruption?" prompt="A caller says the agent spoke over her twice during one call. The agent's LLM and TTS spans all show normal latency and no errors. What single piece of trace evidence would tell you whether the failure was a slow barge-in detector, a buffered audio chunk that couldn't be canceled, or a voice-activity-detection threshold tuned for a quiet room instead of a car?" outcome="You need the interval between the user's first detected speech frame and the timestamp the agent's audio output actually stopped, plus the size of any TTS audio already buffered downstream at that moment. A trace that stops at 'LLM call succeeded' cannot distinguish a detection delay from a playback-cancellation delay — and those two failures have different fixes." /%}

## At a glance

![A five-leg relay race track where a baton is handed from runner to runner for endpoint detection, ASR, the LLM's noticeably longer leg, TTS, and network playback, ending at a finish line labeled user hears the agent](/media/voice-ai-agent-observability-turn-timeline.svg)

This guide is for engineers building or operating a real-time voice agent — a phone-based support line, a voice assistant, or any system where a person talks and the agent talks back with an audible pause in between. It teaches a trace model for one voice **turn** — not one LLM call — and the metrics that expose failures a text-agent dashboard cannot see: dead air, talk-over, mispronunciation, and silent misrecognition. {% highlight %}A voice agent can pass every text-agent health check and still be unusable on a phone call{% /highlight %}, because the pipeline has stages, timing relationships, and interruption behavior that a request/response trace was never built to hold.

The guide does not cover choosing a specific ASR or TTS vendor, telephony/SIP transport engineering, or codec-level audio processing — those are implementation details that sit underneath the trace model, not part of it. For the general trace and span vocabulary this guide extends, see [the AI agent trace schema](/blog/ai-agent-trace-schema); for propagating that vocabulary across services, see [LLM tracing explained](/blog/llm-tracing-explained).

### The CRISP turn model

Name the five stages every voice turn passes through and you have a framework the rest of the article reuses: **Capture → Recognize → Interpret → Synthesize → Play**, or **CRISP**. Barge-in is not a sixth stage — it is a condition that can interrupt any of the other five, which is why it needs its own cross-cutting trace fields rather than a single span.

| Stage | What happens | Primary trace field | Typical failure signature |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Capture | Audio in, voice-activity detection, endpoint decision | `endpoint_detected_at` | Endpointing too early (cuts off a slow talker) or too late (dead air) |
| Recognize | Streaming ASR produces partial and final transcripts | `asr_final_at`, `asr_wer_estimate` | High word-error-rate on accented or noisy audio, wrong final transcript |
| Interpret | LLM reasons over the transcript and decides whether to speak, wait, or yield | `llm_first_token_at`, `turn_decision` | Slow time-to-first-token, wrong turn decision under ambiguity |
| Synthesize | TTS converts the response to audio, often streamed | `tts_first_byte_at` | Slow time-to-first-audio-byte, mispronunciation of a critical word |
| Play | Audio is delivered to the caller, barge-in is possible throughout | `playback_stopped_at`, `bargein_detected_at` | Talk-over, echo self-interruption, audio that never plays |

> **Decision rule:** If a trace cannot answer "which of the five CRISP stages consumed the time, and was barge-in active during any of them," it is not yet a voice trace — it is a text trace with an audio label.

## Why text-agent tracing misses voice failures

A text or chat agent trace is built around a request-response unit: input arrives, the agent reasons, output returns. [The AI agent trace schema](/blog/ai-agent-trace-schema) models this well — a run contains traces, traces contain spans, spans contain events. That model still applies to voice, but a voice turn adds two properties a chat turn does not have: it is **streaming** at every stage, and it is **interruptible** at every stage. A chat message either exists or it doesn't; a spoken utterance can be half-heard, half-spoken, or spoken over.

Three consequences follow. First, latency is not one number — the caller does not experience "the LLM call took 700ms," they experience the accumulated silence from the moment they stopped talking to the moment sound starts coming back, which is the sum of every CRISP stage plus network delivery. [LLM latency optimization](/blog/llm-latency-optimization) covers reducing the model's own contribution, but a voice agent needs the full chain measured, not just the model span.

Second, a "successful" span can still be a conversational failure. The ASR can return a grammatically valid, confidently scored transcript that is simply wrong — a misheard account number, a misheard "not" that flips a yes/no answer. The TTS can synthesize the correct text and mispronounce the one word that mattered, such as a drug name or a dollar amount. None of these register as errors in a conventional trace because nothing threw an exception.

Third, the agent's own audio output is a live, cancelable process, not a returned value. A chat agent's response is complete the moment it's generated. A voice agent's response is still being *played* for as long as several seconds after generation, and during that window the user can interrupt it. If a trace only records when synthesis finished, not when playback actually stopped, it cannot explain a barge-in failure at all.

> **Field note:** Teams that adapt an existing text-agent trace schema to voice by adding a single `duration_ms` field to the TTS span consistently miss barge-in failures, because that field describes synthesis time, not the playback interval during which the user could have interrupted and the system failed to notice.

## Stage 1: Capture — audio in, VAD, and endpointing

### Voice activity detection sets the clock for everything downstream

Capture is where the pipeline decides the user has finished a thought and it is the agent's turn. A voice-activity detector (VAD) watches the incoming audio stream for speech versus silence, and an endpointing policy decides how much trailing silence counts as "done talking" versus "still thinking." This single threshold — commonly 300–800ms of silence — sets the floor for how quickly the agent can respond and the ceiling for how often it cuts someone off mid-sentence.

Trace `endpoint_detected_at` relative to the last frame of detected speech, and record the endpointing policy's configured silence threshold as span metadata. Without that pairing, a support engineer investigating "the agent interrupted me" cannot tell whether the threshold was too aggressive for this call or whether the caller genuinely paused for a long time.

### What to trace at capture

Capture should emit, at minimum: stream start time, first-speech-detected time, endpoint-detected time, the configured silence threshold, and an audio-quality signal such as estimated signal-to-noise ratio. That last field matters more than it looks — a noisy line (car, warehouse, crowded room) degrades every downstream stage, and without a capture-stage quality signal, a high ASR word-error-rate later in the turn looks like an ASR problem when it is actually a capture-stage problem.

## Stage 2: Recognize — streaming ASR and word-error rate

### Partial transcripts are not the same signal as final transcripts

Modern streaming ASR emits a sequence of partial transcripts that get revised as more audio arrives, followed by one final transcript once the endpoint fires. A common instrumentation mistake is logging only the final transcript. Partial transcripts matter for two reasons: some turn-taking logic uses partial transcript stability to predict an interruption is safe to act on, and a large gap between an early partial and the eventual final transcript is itself a signal that the ASR model is struggling with this audio — background noise, cross-talk, or an unfamiliar accent.

Trace every partial transcript as an event on the recognize span with its confidence score and the wall-clock time it arrived, then trace the final transcript separately with `asr_final_at`. This lets a later investigation replay exactly what the system "knew" at each moment, not just what it eventually decided.

### Measuring word-error-rate on real, noisy audio

Word-error-rate (WER) — the edit distance between the recognized transcript and a verified reference, divided by reference length — is the standard ASR quality metric, but it is only useful in production if it is measured on audio that resembles production audio: phone-line compression, background noise, regional accents, code-switching, and interruption artifacts. {% highlight %}A WER benchmark measured on clean, single-speaker studio audio tells you almost nothing about a customer calling from a car with the window down{% /highlight %}.

Build a small held-out sample of real production audio, hand-correct the transcripts, and track WER against that sample by cohort — by language, by detected accent cluster, by reported audio-quality bucket. The comparison method in [the practical AI agent evaluation guide](/blog/ai-agent-evaluation-practical-guide) — matching baseline and candidate on the same task set — applies directly here: compare ASR model versions on the same held-out audio set rather than trusting an aggregate WER number from a vendor's own benchmark.

> **Field note:** A WER of 6% sounds low until you notice it is concentrated entirely in one cohort — callers with a specific regional accent — because the training data underrepresented that accent. An aggregate WER hides exactly the failure a segmented WER reveals.

## Stage 3: Interpret — turn-taking decisions, not just answers

### The LLM's job in a voice agent is bigger than generating a response

In a text agent, the model's job is to produce a good answer. In a voice agent, the model (or a lightweight turn-taking policy layered around it) has a second job: deciding whether the user has actually finished speaking, whether to speak now, wait, or ask a clarifying question, and whether an in-progress user utterance is a real interruption or a filler sound ("um," a cough, a side conversation picked up by the microphone). Getting the *content* right and getting the *timing* right are separate problems, and a trace needs to capture both.

Record `llm_first_token_at` — time-to-first-token — separately from total generation time, because the first stage of TTS can often begin as soon as the first sentence of the response streams out, and time-to-first-token is what determines how much of the overall turn latency budget the interpret stage consumes. Also record the explicit `turn_decision` the system made: respond now, wait for more input, or yield the floor because the user appears to still be talking. Silent failures cluster here — the model decides to respond while the endpoint detector was actually wrong about the user finishing, producing exactly the "talked over the interruption" failure from the opening scenario.

### End-of-turn detection latency

End-of-turn detection latency is the time between the true end of the user's utterance and the moment the system commits to a turn decision. It is distinct from ASR finalization latency, because a system can finalize a transcript quickly and still delay its turn decision waiting for a confidence threshold or a secondary confirmation pass. Measure it directly rather than assuming it equals ASR latency; the two drift apart under load, and that drift is often the first sign of a capacity problem before p95 latency alerts fire.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Operating principle" title="Budget the whole turn, not just the model call." body="Set one latency budget for the full CRISP chain — commonly 1,200–2,000ms from endpoint to first audible response for a natural-feeling conversation — and allocate it across capture, recognize, interpret, and synthesize explicitly. A model call that returns in 400ms is not fast if endpointing already spent 600ms and TTS needs another 500ms before the first audio byte." tone="yellow" /%}

## Stage 4: Synthesize — TTS streaming and the silent mispronunciation

### Time-to-first-audio-byte is the metric that determines whether the agent feels alive

Time-to-first-audio-byte (TTFAB) measures from the moment the interpret stage produces enough text to synthesize through to the first audio byte the playback stage receives. Streaming TTS can start synthesizing and delivering audio before the full response text exists, which is the difference between a caller hearing the agent start speaking in 400ms versus waiting the full 1,500ms for the complete response to generate and then synthesize. {% highlight %}If your TTS integration waits for the complete LLM response before starting synthesis, TTFAB and total turn latency become the same number{% /highlight %}, and that is usually the single largest latency win available in an existing pipeline.

### Silent failures: audio that is valid but conversationally wrong

The most dangerous TTS failures never raise an error. The synthesis succeeds, the audio plays, the caller hears something — and that something is wrong in a way no health check catches. A confirmation number gets mispronounced digit by digit incorrectly. A medication name gets an emphasis pattern that makes it unintelligible. A price gets read in the wrong currency's cadence. These are **conversationally wrong, technically valid** outputs, and they are the voice-agent equivalent of a text agent that returns a well-formatted but factually incorrect answer.

Trace the exact text string sent to the TTS engine alongside the synthesized audio's duration and any engine-reported pronunciation warnings or phoneme substitutions. For a defined list of critical terms — product names, medical terms, monetary amounts, confirmation codes — maintain a pronunciation regression set the same way you would maintain a text-agent regression suite, and replay it against each TTS model or voice version before rollout.

| Failure mode | What the trace shows without voice instrumentation | What the CRISP trace adds |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mispronounced confirmation number | TTS span succeeded, normal duration | Exact source text plus phoneme/pronunciation warning flags |
| Talked over an interruption | LLM and TTS spans both green | Gap between `bargein_detected_at` and `playback_stopped_at` |
| Misheard "not" flips a yes/no answer | ASR confidence score looks acceptable | Partial-to-final transcript delta and WER cohort |
| Long dead air before response | No span errors, total latency within SLO | Per-stage breakdown showing which CRISP stage consumed the budget |

## Stage 5: Play — playback and the barge-in race

### The interruption race condition

Barge-in is fundamentally a race: the system must detect that the user has started speaking, decide that this is a genuine interruption rather than noise, and stop or duck the agent's outgoing audio — all while audio may already be buffered several hundred milliseconds ahead in a playback queue, a jitter buffer, or a telephony provider's own pipeline. {% highlight %}The gap between "system detected the interruption" and "audio actually stopped" is the barge-in response time{% /highlight %}, and it is frequently invisible in a trace that only logs when synthesis completed, not when playback stopped.

Trace three separate timestamps for every turn where barge-in is possible: `bargein_candidate_at` (the moment speech was first detected in the user channel while the agent was still playing audio), `bargein_confirmed_at` (the moment the system decided this was a real interruption, not noise), and `playback_stopped_at` (the moment agent audio actually ceased). The interval between the first and the third is the caller's talk-over experience; the interval between the first and the second isolates detection latency from cancellation latency, and those two intervals point to different fixes.

> **Security note:** Never treat a barge-in event as implicit reauthorization for a sensitive action already in progress — for example, do not let an interruption mid-confirmation silently commit a payment or account change that was still being read back. Barge-in should pause the flow, not fast-forward it.

## A worked example: the agent talks over an interruption

![Two real overlapping waveforms, agent audio on top and user audio below, with a shaded talk-over window marking the gap between the user starting to talk and the agent's waveform actually stopping](/media/voice-ai-agent-observability-barge-in.svg)

Return to the opening scenario. The caller says the agent spoke over her while she tried to correct a wrong account number. The team pulls the CRISP trace instead of the plain LLM/TTS trace, and gets a very different picture.

| Timestamp (relative) | Event | Duration | What it tells the investigator |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| t = 0ms | `endpoint_detected_at` — agent begins its response | — | Turn proceeds normally |
| t = 40ms | `tts_first_byte_at` — synthesis starts streaming | 40ms | Fast TTFAB, not the problem |
| t = 760ms | `bargein_candidate_at` — user speech detected mid-playback | — | The user did interrupt here |
| t = 940ms | `bargein_confirmed_at` — system classifies it as a real interruption | 180ms | Detection-to-confirmation took 180ms |
| t = 1,100ms | `playback_stopped_at` — agent audio actually stops | 160ms after confirmation | Cancellation itself added another 160ms |

The evidence separates two candidate root causes that looked identical from the outside. The 180ms detection-to-confirmation gap is a VAD sensitivity tuning problem — the barge-in classifier required more confirming audio than necessary before accepting the interruption as genuine. The additional 160ms cancellation delay is a different problem entirely: a downstream audio buffer was already queued with roughly one sentence of synthesized speech, and canceling playback meant draining that buffer rather than stopping instantly. Fixing the classifier threshold would not have fixed the buffering delay, and fixing the buffer size alone would not have fixed classifier sensitivity. A trace that only recorded "barge-in happened, response was delayed" would have sent the team chasing the wrong half of a 340ms problem.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Diagnostic shortcut" title="Split every barge-in complaint into detection time and cancellation time before touching code." body="Pull bargein_candidate_at, bargein_confirmed_at, and playback_stopped_at for the affected turn. If detection-to-confirmation dominates, tune the VAD/classifier. If confirmation-to-stop dominates, shrink the playback buffer or add mid-stream cancellation. Guessing which one it is from the caller's description alone is close to a coin flip." tone="dark" /%}

## The voice observability metrics that matter

A useful voice-agent dashboard tracks a small set of metrics that map directly to the CRISP stages, not a generic latency-and-error-rate pair borrowed from a web service dashboard.

| Metric | Stage | What good looks like | What a regression usually means |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Endpointing accuracy | Capture | Low rate of premature cutoffs and long dead-air gaps | Silence threshold miscalibrated for the channel or environment |
| Word-error-rate by cohort | Recognize | Stable WER across accent, language, and noise cohorts | A specific cohort underrepresented in training data |
| End-of-turn detection latency | Interpret | Consistent, not just low on average | Load-related drift ahead of a wider latency SLO breach |
| Time-to-first-audio-byte (TTFAB) | Synthesize | Low enough that the pause feels natural, not mechanical | Non-streaming synthesis path or oversized response before speaking |
| Barge-in response time | Play | Short gap between detected interruption and stopped audio | Detection threshold too conservative, or an oversized playback buffer |
| Turn latency (p50/p95) | All | p95 close to p50; no long tail | One stage occasionally stalling under real network or provider conditions |

Track p95, not only mean, for every latency metric in this table. {% highlight %}A voice agent that is fast on average but has a fat tail of 4-second turns is worse for the caller than one that is uniformly 1.8 seconds{% /highlight %}, because the caller cannot tell in the moment whether a long pause means the agent is thinking or has failed silently.

## Diagnosing common voice pipeline failure patterns

### Silence storms and dead air

A "silence storm" is a run of turns where the endpoint detector repeatedly waits the full silence threshold without a good reason — often because a noise floor changed (a door opened, traffic noise increased) and the VAD's sensitivity no longer matches the room. The symptom is dead air the caller experiences as the agent "not responding." Trace the capture-stage signal-to-noise estimate alongside endpoint latency; a spike in silence-to-endpoint duration that correlates with a drop in estimated SNR points at the environment, not the model.

### Echo and self-interruption

If the agent's own outgoing audio leaks into the microphone input — common on speakerphone or with imperfect echo cancellation — the barge-in detector can classify the agent's own voice as a user interruption, causing the agent to stop mid-sentence for no external reason. {% highlight %}Trace whether a detected interruption correlates in time and frequency with the agent's own currently-playing audio{% /highlight %}; a high correlation points to echo, not a genuine barge-in, and should route to an acoustic-echo-cancellation investigation rather than a turn-taking policy change.

### Language and accent drift in ASR

Cohort-level WER regressions often show up gradually as a caller population shifts — a new market opens, a marketing campaign shifts the caller mix, a holiday period brings in less-frequent callers with different speech patterns. Without cohort-segmented tracking, this drift is invisible until aggregate quality metrics move enough to trigger an alert, by which point the affected cohort has had a degraded experience for weeks.

## Instrumenting a voice-turn trace schema

Extending a standard trace/span model for voice means adding stage-specific timestamps and turn-level fields without abandoning the run/trace/span/event shape that already works for text agents. The schema below is illustrative — adapt field names to your existing trace schema rather than adopting this one wholesale.

```ts
// Illustrative: extend an existing trace/span schema with voice-turn fields.
// Verify field names against your production schema before using this shape.
interface VoiceTurnSpan {
  turnId: string;
  callId: string;

  // Stage 1 — Capture
  speechStartedAt: number;
  endpointDetectedAt: number;
  silenceThresholdMs: number;
  estimatedSnrDb?: number;

  // Stage 2 — Recognize
  asrPartials: Array<{ text: string; confidence: number; atMs: number }>;
  asrFinalAt: number;
  asrFinalText: string;
  asrWerEstimate?: number;

  // Stage 3 — Interpret
  llmFirstTokenAt: number;
  llmResponseCompleteAt: number;
  turnDecision: "respond" | "wait" | "yield";

  // Stage 4 — Synthesize
  ttsFirstByteAt: number;
  ttsSourceText: string;
  pronunciationWarnings?: string[];

  // Stage 5 — Play, including barge-in (cross-cutting, optional)
  playbackStartedAt: number;
  playbackStoppedAt?: number;
  bargeIn?: {
    candidateAt: number;
    confirmedAt: number;
    playbackStoppedAt: number;
  };
}
```

### What belongs on a span versus an event

Keep per-stage timestamps and summary fields — `asrFinalAt`, `ttsFirstByteAt`, `turnDecision` — as span attributes, since they describe one occurrence per turn. Keep the sequence of ASR partial transcripts and any barge-in candidate detections as ordered events within the span, since there can be zero, one, or several of each per turn and an investigator needs to replay their order. This mirrors the general run/span/event separation in [the AI agent trace schema](/blog/ai-agent-trace-schema): spans describe a bounded unit of work, events describe things that happened during it.

## Protect the audio privacy boundary

### What must never be logged

Voice agents create a category of sensitive data a text agent does not: raw audio recordings of a caller's actual voice, which can contain account numbers, medical information, or other regulated content spoken aloud, plus biometric characteristics of the voice itself. {% highlight %}Treat full-call audio recordings the way you would treat unencrypted credentials{% /highlight %} — access-controlled, encrypted at rest, and never routed into a general-purpose logging or trace-search system where any engineer with dashboard access can retrieve them. Trace fields should carry timestamps, durations, confidence scores, and stage outcomes; they should not carry raw audio bytes or unredacted transcripts by default.

> **Security note:** If a debugging workflow genuinely requires audio playback, gate it behind a separate, audited access path with a narrower retention window than your general trace store — not the same trace UI an on-call engineer opens for a routine latency investigation.

### Retention boundaries for recordings

Define a retention window for raw audio that is shorter than your trace-metadata retention window, and make the difference explicit to the team: trace metadata (timestamps, WER, turn decisions) can reasonably persist as long as other observability data, but raw audio and full transcripts carry materially higher risk per day retained. Where regulation applies to the call type — healthcare, financial account verification — retention and consent requirements should be set by your compliance function, not inferred from what the observability tooling happens to store by default.

## Trade-offs: streaming complexity versus observability overhead

Every additional trace field on the hot path of a real-time voice pipeline has a cost: serialization time, network payload size, and — if instrumentation itself blocks the audio pipeline even briefly — a direct latency regression in the metric you are trying to protect. This is a sharper version of a trade-off every observability system faces, but voice makes it acute because the pipeline is latency-sensitive at the level of individual milliseconds, not the hundreds-of-milliseconds tolerance a typical API endpoint has.

The practical resolution is asynchronous, buffered instrumentation: write trace events to a local buffer and flush them off the hot path, never synchronously call out to a tracing backend before releasing an audio frame or a token. Sample full partial-transcript event streams for a subset of calls rather than every call, while keeping stage-summary timestamps (endpoint, ASR final, first token, TTFAB, barge-in) on every call — those summary fields are cheap and carry most of the diagnostic value. [LLM latency optimization](/blog/llm-latency-optimization) covers the same buffered-instrumentation principle for text agents; the tolerance for getting it wrong is simply much smaller in a voice pipeline where a 50ms instrumentation stall is audible as a stutter.

> **Decision rule:** Sample expensive, high-volume trace data (raw partial transcripts, full audio snapshots); never sample the cheap, high-value summary timestamps that make a turn diagnosable. If a budget cut has to happen, cut volume before cutting coverage.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Decision room" title="Ship the five summary timestamps before the full event stream." body="A team under time pressure should instrument endpoint_detected_at, asr_final_at, llm_first_token_at, tts_first_byte_at, and playback_stopped_at first — full CRISP stage coverage with none of the partial-transcript or barge-in-candidate detail. That alone turns 'the agent felt slow' into a stage-attributable investigation. Add barge-in sub-timestamps and partial-transcript events once the base five are flowing reliably." tone="paper" /%}

## Ship it: a practical instrumentation checklist

- [ ] Every turn records the five CRISP-stage summary timestamps: endpoint detected, ASR final, LLM first token, TTS first byte, playback stopped.
- [ ] Barge-in turns additionally record candidate, confirmed, and playback-stopped timestamps as three distinct fields, not one collapsed "interrupted" flag.
- [ ] Word-error-rate is tracked by cohort (language, accent cluster, reported audio quality), not only as a single aggregate number.
- [ ] TTS source text and any pronunciation warnings are traced alongside audio duration, so a mispronunciation can be investigated without needing to re-listen to the call.
- [ ] Raw audio and unredacted transcripts sit behind a narrower-retention, access-controlled path separate from general trace search.
- [ ] Trace writes are buffered and flushed off the real-time audio path, never synchronously blocking a frame or token.
- [ ] p95 turn latency, not just mean, is on the primary dashboard, with a per-stage breakdown available from the same view.
- [ ] At least one barge-in scenario and one noisy-audio scenario are covered by a repeatable test call before each release.

{% highlight %}A voice trace earns its keep the first time it turns "the agent felt off" into a specific stage and a specific millisecond gap{% /highlight %} — that is the bar to hold it to, not whether every field in the schema above is populated on day one.

Carry this checklist into your release process the same way [LLM tracing explained](/blog/llm-tracing-explained) treats propagation correctness: as a gate before rollout, not a retrospective exercise after the first complaint arrives. Start with the five summary timestamps, add barge-in and cohort-level ASR tracking next, and expand only when a real investigation needs a field the current schema does not have.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="What is the single most important trace field to add first for a voice agent?" answer="Time-to-first-audio-byte, paired with the four other CRISP-stage summary timestamps (endpoint detected, ASR final, LLM first token, playback stopped). Together they turn any 'the agent felt slow or wrong' report into a stage-attributable investigation instead of a guess." /%}
{% faq-item question="How is voice agent tracing different from tracing a text-based chat agent?" answer="A voice turn is streaming and interruptible at every stage, while a chat message is a discrete, non-interruptible unit. Voice tracing needs partial-transcript events, barge-in sub-timestamps, and playback-stop timing that a text-agent trace schema has no equivalent for." /%}
{% faq-item question="Should I log full call recordings for debugging?" answer="Only behind a separate, access-controlled, audited path with a shorter retention window than your general trace store. Everyday debugging should rely on timestamps, confidence scores, and stage outcomes, not raw audio, which carries materially higher privacy risk per day retained." /%}
{% faq-item question="How often should word-error-rate be reviewed?" answer="Continuously by cohort, not just as a monthly aggregate. Segment by language, accent cluster, and reported audio quality, and review before and after any ASR model or vendor change using the same held-out, hand-corrected audio sample so comparisons are like-for-like." /%}
{% faq-item question="Does adding this much instrumentation slow the voice pipeline down?" answer="Only if it is implemented synchronously on the hot path. Buffer trace writes and flush them asynchronously, and sample expensive high-volume data such as full partial-transcript streams while keeping the cheap per-turn summary timestamps on every call. The summary fields carry most of the diagnostic value at a fraction of the cost." /%}
